sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Given a segment of a circle, to describe the complete circle of which it is a segment.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCseg} be the given segment of a circle.

It is required to describe the complete circle belonging to the segment {ABC|ABCseg}, that is, of which it is a segment.

Bisect {AC} at {D}, draw {DB} from the point {D} at right angles to {AC}, and join {AB}. [!just I.10; I.11]

<figure class="diagram" data-spans="4">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.25a diagram" src="propIII25a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Three canvases, one per case of the trichotomy at B. This is the first
    // case (angle ABD greater, centre E outside the segment), which carries
    // the full argument; canvas_1 (semicircle) and canvas_2 (greater than a
    // semicircle) each carry Joyce's short case sentence as a walk of their
    // own. Prose refs after canvas_1 that still talk about this figure name
    // it explicitly ({AE:canvas_0}), since DOM order would bind them to the
    // nearest preceding canvas.
    //
    // Joyce draws the segment as the sector E,A,C with a random face, then
    // paints the triangle ACE in the background colour to leave only the
    // segment coloured. That sector is therefore NEVER highlighted — a
    // highlighted element is promoted within its draw pass (#140) and the
    // whole wedge would show through the triangle — and the transparent
    // twin ABCseg carries the name for highlight and hover ({ABC|ABCseg}).
    //
    // As converted, `DB` was the invisible perpendicular carrier for the
    // slider B. It is renamed DB' (the II.1 idiom for BF') so that DB can be
    // the line the proof draws: a black D–B stroke coincident with the D–B
    // stretch of BE, which is Joyce's only line through those points but
    // arrives four slides later than "draw DB" (the II.2 idiom). The rest
    // of the additions are transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0 sector /
    // circle) or angle markers (hidden in the static figure), so the
    // resting figure is unchanged. The right angles at D are red, the
    // Book II convention.
    //
    // The completed circle is not in Joyce's figure, so Ecirc is
    // transparent: gold while its compass animation runs, gold again on
    // the slides that highlight it, invisible at rest.
    var SEG   = ["A","C","AC","B","ABC","ACE","ABCseg"];
    var BIS   = SEG.concat(["D","DB","AB"]);
    var ANG   = BIS.concat(["angABD","angBAD"]);
    var CON   = ANG.concat(["E","AE","angBAE"]);
    var EXT   = CON.concat(["BE","CE"]);
    var SIDES = EXT.concat(["AD","DC","DE"]);
    var RT    = SIDES.concat(["angADE","angCDE"]);
    var CIRC  = RT.concat(["Ecirc"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.25a",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;70,30",
            "C;point;free;70,200",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;midpoint;AC",
            "DB';line;perpendicular;D,A;0;0;0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;DB',40,115",
            "E;point;circumcenter;A,B,C;black;green",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E",
            "ABC;sector;sector;E,A,C;0;0;black;random",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "ACE;polygon;triangle;A,C,E;0;0;0;random",
            // The line the proof draws at D; coincident with BE's D–B stretch.
            "DB;line;connect;D,B",
            // Transparent twin of the segment, for highlight and hover.
            "ABCseg;sector;sector;E,A,C;0;0;0;0",
            // Halves of AC and the common side DE; AC and BE draw them.
            "AD;line;connect;A,D;0;0;0",
            "DC;line;connect;D,C;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            // The angles the proof compares. ABE shares its rays with ABD
            // (D lies on BE), so one marker serves both names.
            "angABD;sector;angleMarker;B,A,D",
            "angBAD;sector;angleMarker;A,B,D",
            "angBAE;sector;angleMarker;A,B,E",
            // Same explicit radius for both, so they opt out of the same-vertex
            // ring-stepping and read as two quarter-discs split by DE.
            "angADE;sector;angleMarker;D,A,E,22;0;0;red;red",
            "angCDE;sector;angleMarker;D,C,E,22;0;0;red;red",
            // The completed circle.
            "Ecirc;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        // A, C (free) and B (lineSlider) are the given segment's points, all
        // introduced on slide 1, so nothing is deferred. D (midpoint) and E
        // (circumcenter) are derived and NOT auto-unioned, so each is listed
        // from the slide that introduces it.
        aliases: { "BA": "AB", "CD": "DC", "DA": "AD", "BD": "DB", "ED": "DE",
                   "EB": "BE", "EA": "AE", "EC": "CE",
                   "ABD": "angABD", "ABE": "angABD", "BAD": "angBAD",
                   "BAE": "angBAE", "ADE": "angADE", "CDE": "angCDE" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”).
            { text: "Given a segment of a circle, to describe the complete circle of which it is a segment.",
              visible: SEG,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.25" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABC|ABCseg} be the given segment of a circle.",
              visible: SEG,
              highlighted: ["ABCseg"] },
            { text: "It is required to describe the complete circle belonging to the segment {ABC|ABCseg}, that is, of which it is a segment.",
              visible: SEG,
              highlighted: ["ABCseg"] },
            { text: "Bisect {AC} at {D}, draw {DB} from the point {D} at right angles to {AC}, and join {AB}.",
              visible: BIS,
              highlighted: ["AC", "D", "DB", "AB"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "D",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "DB", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "AB", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "AC bisected at D",           ref: "I.10" },
                  { claim: "DB at right angles to AC",   ref: "I.11" } ] },
            { text: "The angle {ABD} is then greater than, equal to, or less than the angle {BAD}.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBAD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "First let it be greater.",
              visible: ANG,
              highlighted: ["angABD"] },
            { text: "Construct the angle {BAE} on the straight line {BA}, and at the point {A} on it, equal to the angle {ABD}.",
              visible: CON,
              highlighted: ["angABD"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "E",      name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "angBAE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "angle BAE constructed equal to angle ABD", ref: "I.23" }] },
            { text: "Draw {DB} through to {E}, and join {EC}.",
              visible: EXT,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "CE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.2" }, { ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Then, since the angle {ABE} equals the angle {BAE}, the straight line {EB} also equals {EA}.",
              visible: EXT,
              highlighted: ["angABD","angBAE","BE","AE"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "equal angles are subtended by equal sides", ref: "I.6" }] },
            { text: "And, since {AD} equals {DC}, and {DE} is common, the two sides {AD} and {DE} equal the two sides {CD} and {DE} respectively,",
              visible: SIDES,
              highlighted: ["AD","DC","DE"] },
            { text: "and the angle {ADE} equals the angle {CDE}, for each is right, therefore the base {AE} equals the base {CE}.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AE","CE"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angADE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angCDE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "two sides and the included right angle", ref: "I.4" }] },
            { text: "But {AE} was proved equal to {BE}, therefore be also equals {CE}.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AE","BE","CE"] },
            { text: "Therefore the three straight lines {AE}, {EB}, and {EC} equal one another.",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["AE","BE","CE"] },
            { text: "Therefore the circle drawn with center {E} and radius one of the straight lines {AE}, {EB}, or {EC} also passes through the remaining points and has been completed.",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: ["E", "Ecirc"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "Ecirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "three equal straight lines from E", ref: "III.9" }] },
            { text: "Therefore, given a segment of a circle, the complete circle has been described.",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: ["Ecirc","E"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] },
            { text: "And it is manifest that the segment {ABC|ABCseg} is less than a semicircle, because the center {E} happens to be outside it.",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: ["ABCseg","E"] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.25") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification,
    // shared by all three canvases on this page.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Post.2": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post2/",
        "I.4":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI4/",
        "I.6":      "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI6/",
        "I.10":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI10/",
        "I.11":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI11/",
        "I.23":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI23/",
        "III.9":    "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII9/"
    });
    // E sits near the middle of the canvas, where the default label rule
    // (away from the canvas centre) picks a side almost at random and put
    // the letter on the line BD. Fix it right, clear of the lines at E.
    (function () {
        var sl = geomlib.slates[geomlib.slates.length - 1];
        var e = sl && sl.lookupElement("E");
        if (e) { e.align = geomlib.Align.RIGHT; sl.update(); }
    })();
</script>
</figure>

The angle {ABD} is then greater than, equal to, or less than the angle {BAD}.

First let it be greater.  Construct the angle {BAE} on the straight line {BA}, and at the point {A} on it, equal to the angle {ABD}.  Draw {DB} through to {E}, and join {EC}. [!just I.23]

Then, since the angle {ABE} equals the angle {BAE}, the straight line {EB} also equals {EA}. [!just I.6]

And, since {AD} equals {DC}, and {DE} is common, the two sides {AD} and {DE} equal the two sides {CD} and {DE} respectively, and the angle {ADE} equals the angle {CDE}, for each is right, therefore the base {AE} equals the base {CE}. [!just I.4]

<figure class="diagram rdiagram" data-spans="5">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="250" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.25b diagram" src="propIII25b.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The second case: angle ABD equal to angle BAD, D the centre, ABC a
    // semicircle. Joyce's sentence for it is walked here in three beats.
    // Additions are transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0 circle) or angle
    // markers (hidden in the static figure); the resting figure is
    // unchanged.
    //
    // The arc ABC has NO Sector.sweep: at exactly 180 degrees the two arm
    // orders are the two different semicircles, and the order that draws
    // Joyce's side sweeps backwards (euclid#172) — measured against the
    // bundle, ink at t=0.5 exceeds t=1. It is revealed without sweeping.
    var S1 = ["A","C","AC","D","DB","B","ABC","AB","angABD","angBAD"];
    var S2 = S1.concat(["AD","DC"]);
    var S3 = S2.concat(["Dcirc"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.25b",
        pivot: "D",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;170,45",
            "C;point;free;170,185",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;midpoint;AC;black;green",
            "DB;line;perpendicular;D,A",
            "B;point;last;DB",
            "ABC;sector;arc;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            // Halves of AC; AC draws them.
            "AD;line;connect;A,D;0;0;0",
            "DC;line;connect;D,C;0;0;0",
            // The two base angles, and the completed circle about D.
            "angABD;sector;angleMarker;B,A,D",
            "angBAD;sector;angleMarker;A,B,D",
            "Dcirc;circle;radius;D,A;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        // A and C are the only draggables and both are givens; B (last) and
        // D (midpoint) are derived and listed explicitly.
        aliases: { "BD": "DB", "DA": "AD", "CD": "DC", "BA": "AB",
                   "ABD": "angABD", "BAD": "angBAD" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Similarly, even if the angle {ABD} equals the angle {BAD}",
              visible: S1,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBAD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and {AD} being equal to each of the two {BD} and {DC}, the three straight lines {DA}, {DB}, and {DC} will equal one another,",
              visible: S2,
              highlighted: ["AD","DB","DC"] },
            { text: "{D} will be the center of the completed circle, and {ABC} will clearly be a semicircle.",
              visible: S3,
              highlighted: ["D", "ABC", "Dcirc"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "Dcirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] } }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
</script>
</figure>
<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_2" width="250" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.25c diagram" src="propIII25c.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The third case: angle ABD less than angle BAD, the centre E on DB
    // inside the segment, ABC greater than a semicircle. Joyce's sentence
    // for it is walked here, closing with the Q.E.F. that ends his page.
    //
    // The sector ABC is a 4-field element as converted — no colour fields,
    // so an opaque near-white default face (Joyce's gif fills it green; the
    // conversion lost that, and it is left as it is). The triangle ACE
    // likewise has no face field. Neither is ever highlighted: a
    // highlighted element is promoted within its pass (#140), and the white
    // wedge would cover the triangle. The transparent twin ABCseg, on the
    // same arms, carries the name for highlight and hover ({ABC|ABCseg});
    // ABC itself appears only in `visible`, where it draws as it always did.
    // Other additions are angle markers (hidden in the static figure) and a
    // transparent circle, so the resting figure is unchanged. As converted,
    // `DB` was the invisible perpendicular carrier for the slider B; it is
    // renamed DB' so the prose's "DB" can resolve to the drawn line BD.
    var T1 = ["A","C","AC","D","B","BD","AB","ABC","ACE","ABCseg","angABD","angBAD"];
    var T2 = T1.concat(["E","AE","angBAE"]);
    var T3 = T2.concat(["Ecirc"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_2",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.25c",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;200,30",
            "C;point;free;200,200",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "D;point;midpoint;AC",
            "DB';line;perpendicular;D,A;0;0;0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;DB',30,115",
            "E;point;circumcenter;A,B,C;black;green",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "ABC;sector;sector;E,A,C;0;0;black;random",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "ACE;polygon;triangle;A,C,E;0;0;0;random",
            // Transparent twin of the 4-field sector (see above).
            "ABCseg;sector;sector;E,A,C;0;0;0;0",
            // The angles compared, the constructed angle, the completed circle.
            "angABD;sector;angleMarker;B,A,D",
            "angBAD;sector;angleMarker;A,B,D",
            "angBAE;sector;angleMarker;A,B,E",
            "Ecirc;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        // A, C (free) and B (lineSlider) are the given segment's points;
        // nothing is deferred. D and E are derived and listed explicitly.
        aliases: { "BA": "AB", "DB": "BD", "ABD": "angABD", "BAD": "angBAD",
                   "BAE": "angBAE" },
        slides: [
            { text: "But, if the angle {ABD} is less than the angle {BAD},",
              visible: T1,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBAD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and if we construct, on the straight line {BA} and at the point {A} on it, an angle equal to the angle {ABD},",
              visible: T2,
              highlighted: ["angABD", "AE", "E", "angBAE"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "E",      name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "angBAE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "an angle constructed equal to angle ABD", ref: "I.23" }] },
            { text: "the center will fall on {DB} within the segment {ABC|ABCseg}, and the segment {ABC|ABCseg} will clearly be greater than a semicircle.",
              visible: T2,
              highlighted: ["E","BD","ABCseg"] },
            { text: "Therefore, given a segment of a circle, the complete circle has been described.",
              visible: T3,
              highlighted: ["E", "Ecirc"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "Ecirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
    // E sits near the middle of the canvas, where the default label rule
    // (away from the canvas centre) picks a side almost at random and put
    // the letter on the line BD. Fix it below, clear of the lines at E.
    (function () {
        var sl = geomlib.slates[geomlib.slates.length - 1];
        var e = sl && sl.lookupElement("E");
        if (e) { e.align = geomlib.Align.BELOW; sl.update(); }
    })();
</script>
</figure>

But {AE:canvas_0} was proved equal to {BE:canvas_0}, therefore be also equals {CE:canvas_0}.  Therefore the three straight lines {AE:canvas_0}, {EB:canvas_0}, and {EC:canvas_0} equal one another.

Therefore the circle drawn with center {E:canvas_0} and radius one of the straight lines {AE:canvas_0}, {EB:canvas_0}, or {EC:canvas_0} also passes through the remaining points and has been completed. [!just III.9]

Therefore, given a segment of a circle, the complete circle has been described.

<hr width="30%">

And it is manifest that the segment {ABC|ABCseg:canvas_0} is less than a semicircle, because the center {E:canvas_0} happens to be outside it.

Similarly, even if the angle {ABD:canvas_1} equals the angle {BAD:canvas_1} and {AD:canvas_1} being equal to each of the two {BD:canvas_1} and {DC:canvas_1}, the three straight lines {DA:canvas_1}, {DB:canvas_1}, and {DC:canvas_1} will equal one another, {D:canvas_1} will be the center of the completed circle, and {ABC:canvas_1} will clearly be a semicircle.

<hr width="30%">

But, if the angle {ABD} is less than the angle {BAD}, and if we construct, on the straight line {BA} and at the point {A} on it, an angle equal to the angle {ABD}, the center will fall on {DB} within the segment {ABC|ABCseg}, and the segment {ABC|ABCseg} will clearly be greater than a semicircle. [!just I.23]

Therefore, given a segment of a circle, the complete circle has been described.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
<br clear="all">
----
guide: The construction in this proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
